is net worth peaked in 1913 at um 900  million dollars  which if you translate into contemporary  dollars is 13 billion which does not  sound like very much but doesn't begin  to tell the whole story for a number of  reasons  he had 900 million dollars in 1913 the  entire federal budget in 1913 was 715  billion dollars so he could have  personally  paid for every federal employee in  expense and had money left to spare them  the total accumulated national debt that  year was 1.2 billion so he could have  retired three quarters of the total  national debt rockefeller made the  statement in 1917 he had given away so  much money he said that if he had kept  the money  by 1917 he would have been worth 3  billion which would be more than 30  billion  today  and would put him in second place behind  bill gates but bill gates's wealth only  represents one half of one percent of  the gross national product rockefeller's  wealth represented two and a half  percent of the gross national product  which is why um people claim with some  justice that john d rockefeller was the  richest man in american history  his grandson winthrop went on to be  governor of arkansas his grandson am is  a grandson  nelson went on to be governor of new  york and vice president united states  his great grandson went on to be a  united states senator you said he didn't  like politicians uh would any of those  men been in those positions if they  didn't have his money  i don't know it's a fair question but  one of the interesting things is how the  rockefeller attitude towards politics  changes because when the federal  government brings the big anti-trust  suit against standard at the time teddy  roosevelt rockefeller has a very very  defiant and rather contemptuous attitude  toward government  his son john d rockefeller jr is very  much inspired i think by the atmosphere  of woodrow wilson is interested in  public private partnerships he he buys  up the grand tetons valley  and gives it to the federal government  for what is today you know arguably the  most glorious national park in the  united states did over acadia national  park up in maine and then you actually  have nelson running for office and  becoming governor of arkansas  winthrop becoming a governor of um i'm  sorry nelson became governor of new york  went through became governor of  arkansas  when nelson first entertained uh the  notion of becoming governor of new york  thomas e dewey said he couldn't be  elected dog catcher in new york the  rockefeller name was considered that  much of a liability so while the wealth  was enormously  advantageous the the name itself  was both a help  and a hindrance because the rockefeller  name historically had inspired a lot of  hostility  what if this book is new you mentioned  the english interview before what else  did you find that that hadn't been done  before  one of the things that i did is that all  sorts of things that happened at the  standard oil which john d rockefeller  claimed had never been  had never happened or if they had  happened that had been carried out by  overly zealous underlings i was able to  directly  connect him to all of these various  nefarious activities that went on at  standard oil  to his credit i was able to correct  connect him much more directly with all  of his great philanthropic enterprises  because a lot of people felt that he was  detached from them whereas in fact he  was very actively shaping and monitoring  them  i think perhaps the single most  important thing that i did in this book  was i took somebody  who was considered  inhuman  and turned him into a human being  he was a deeply flawed human being but i  think that i bring him at least i hope i  bring him to life on the on the page  because he had been seen  as almost a kind of malevolent  automaton  uh throughout his his life and he was a  very  if you read the earlier biographies um  he's almost missing from his own bog  he's so ghostly he's so elusive so 