le  it seems out of context with everything  else that and here's the picture i  showed it earlier uh that uh when he was  running around in the countryside in the  car you like to uh  will you explain it this is very very  interesting rockefeller's wife who had  been a sort of professional invalid for  the last 10 or 20 years of her life  after she dies in 1915  john d lives another  22 years there's no evidence during all  the years that his wife was in invalid  that he ever cheated on her he seems to  have been a model  husband one of many contradictions of of  the man a model husband the second she  dies he never has shown interest in  women  uh he's suddenly surrounded by uh women  oh yeah there's uh his his wife in a in  a wheelchair uh she was confined to a  wheelchair during the last few years of  her life as soon as she dies  um he develops a real roving eye and he  um  has this afternoon ritual where he goes  out with a party of people in this large  touring car  he always sits tightly wedged between  two bucks and women on either side  he has a blanket that he draws over  their laps and up to their necks and  his hot itchy fingers would stray under  the the blanket and so  rockefeller lived his adolescence in his  last years whereas when he's a young man  he looks very old and serious when he's  an old man he looks rather jaundy and  and young so he seems to keep getting  younger and even slightly silly as he  gets older how did you learn that the  last little the tail actually there was  a rather gossipy memoir that was um  written by his  uh chief gardener because when they  would go out on these afternoon drives  the gardener would often be driving the  second car  and he said one day they pulled up at a  uh stop sign  and uh this young woman who was sitting  next to rockefeller leapt out of the the  back seat and came running back to his  car and she's got in the  car and she said that old rooster  i'll never sit next to him again and  apparently rockefeller had been trying  to  to feel her up during the the ride one  last little connecting point here that  uh james thomas flexner's father who was  on this show is right here  right that's simon flexner he was the  first director of the rockefeller  institute for medical research today the  rockefeller university and when that was  started in 1901 it was a very very novel  concept rockefeller himself suggested it  it was novel because there was no  institute devoted purely to medical  research and at the time  the idea of paying scientists to sit  around and just daydream and come up  with discoveries was considered  something not only  brand new but rather  quixotic and even doomed to failure but  simon flexner very quickly developed a  treatment a serum for for meningitis and  it's been one of the great success  stories not only of the rockefeller  family but of american medicine  any plans for another book already for  you  i think the next one will have to be  shorter i'm a bit exhausted i've done uh  i've published three monsters  um in a ten year period plus one  the warburgs in the house of morgan are  still very much in in paperback and then  i did a short collection of essays last  year called the um the death of the  banker so i think the next project will  be something shorter and more in the  area of instant gratification what was  the most fun about doing this book  the most fun about doing it i think was  just penetrating all of his disguises  you know there are certain  biographical uh subjects who seem to  live for their biographers they record  all their thoughts and then there are  other maddening people who live in order  to torment and frustrate their  biographers and john d was the supreme  example of this type of secretive person  and i don't know that i penetrated all  of his disguises  for his  mysteries but  enough so that i felt that i really  created a fresh  portrait of the man where'd you get the  title  the title titan well you know 